    "body": "EICMA 2026 • MTPeak Stole Some of the Spotlight at EICMA 2026 With Insane Power and Retro Looks on Three Wheels\nAll-Black Aptera Is the World’s First Production Solar Electric Vehicle, Heading to Indonesia\n15 photos\nPhoto: Aptera\nIt's been a long time coming, but here we finally are, ready to tell the world that its first-ever production vehicle powered by the Sun is here. It's called the Aptera, after the name of the company making it, comes in matte black, and is bound not for the U.S., where it is built, but for Indonesia.\nAptera Motors announced at the end of last week that the first vehicle meant for a customer rolled off the lines at its facility in Carlsbad, California. The buyer, we're told, is Indonesian businessman Martin Hartono, who also happens to be an investor who has backed Aptera's plans for such a contraption.\nYou see, as most other startups out there, Aptera's business model relied a lot on investors. In the company speak, they are called Accelerators, and Hartono is number 1, based on the amount of money invested – hence the honor of him receiving the first vehicle.\nThe subsequent vehicles are reserved for the other Accelerators, but the company does not specify who or how many of them are out there. What's clear, though, is that their number is far lower than the 50,000 people the company claims have already reserved their own solar-powered cars.\nThe three-wheeled vehicle is officially classified as an autocycle, and it was presented in its current form at the 2025 Consumer Electronics Show (CES) in Las Vegas. It is powered by an electric powertrain comprising a 40-kWh liquid-cooled battery and an electric motor. Nothing spectacular here, but the way the battery is charged kind of is.\nOk, one can charge the thing the old-fashioned way, by plugging it into a charging station, as it comes equipped with a North American Charging System (NACS) connector, but they can also use the solar panels installed on the roof for that.\nThere are 700 watts-worth of solar cells up there, and they can send more than four kWh of solar energy into the battery under ideal conditions. That's enough to allow the vehicle to travel for as many as 40 miles (64 km) on free, solar power alone. The total distance the battery is good for is rated at 400 miles (644 km).\nThe electric motor that gets its juice from the battery allows for an acceleration time of six seconds from zero to 60 mph, while the top speed, with the maximum of two people on board, stands at 100 mph (161 kph).\nThe carbon fiber body of the Aptera wraps around a cabin fitted with only a steering yoke, a center display, and a couple of additional displays tied into external cameras, which act as side rearview mirrors.\nThere are some safety features included in the design, including a safety cell, an energy-absorbing front nose cone, and a subframe designed to crumple on impact, but no airbags, ABS, or any driver assistance feature. All of that for a starting price of around $40,000.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nAptera solar panels solar electric car\nAbout the author: Daniel Patrascu\nDaniel loves writing (or so he claims), and he uses this skill to offer readers a \"behind the scenes\" look at the automotive industry. He also enjoys talking about space exploration and robots, because in his view the only way forward for humanity is away from this planet, in metal bodies. Full profile\nUP NEXT EREVs May Dodge EU Hybrid Caps but Still Face BEV Tariffs",
